WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.734 --> 00:00:02.202
- My friend says,

00:00:02.202 --> 00:00:04.404 align:start size:97% position:3%
"Hey, my sister brought
back all these free posters.

00:00:04.404 --> 00:00:05.939
You want some?"

00:00:05.939 --> 00:00:07.707 align:start size:84% position:16%
Now, why would the
Soviet Union give away

00:00:07.707 --> 00:00:12.278
free propaganda posters to
naive stupid Westerners?

00:00:12.278 --> 00:00:14.247 align:start size:88% position:13%
So I remember-
- You can't imagine why.

00:00:14.247 --> 00:00:15.982 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Choosing one of
Lennon and thinking,

00:00:15.982 --> 00:00:17.417
"Well, that sort of looks okay."

00:00:17.417 --> 00:00:19.753 align:start size:97% position:3%
And I put it up, I was living
off campus, in my room.

00:00:19.753 --> 00:00:23.723
My mom and dad came to visit
and my father looked at it,

00:00:23.723 --> 00:00:25.158
and he looked at me and he said,

00:00:25.158 --> 00:00:28.461
"Eric, could I speak
with you for a minute?"

00:00:28.461 --> 00:00:30.296
And it suddenly dawned on me.

00:00:30.296 --> 00:00:32.832
My father said, "I
wanna ask you favor.

00:00:32.832 --> 00:00:34.934
Would you take that down?"

00:00:34.934 --> 00:00:37.170 align:start size:88% position:13%
And it was as if the
scales fell from my eyes

00:00:37.170 --> 00:00:40.407
and I realized what
has happened to me.

00:00:40.407 --> 00:00:41.674 align:start size:84% position:16%
There are a hundred
other examples

00:00:41.674 --> 00:00:44.177
but those are the two
moments that I remember

00:00:44.177 --> 00:00:47.080
suddenly coming to
myself and thinking,

00:00:47.080 --> 00:00:50.383 align:start size:97% position:3%
how is it possible that I
thought it was okay to do that

00:00:50.383 --> 00:00:53.253
when my father raised
me to despise communism

00:00:54.687 --> 00:00:58.324
the way Solzhenitsyn and
Buckley despised communism?

00:00:58.324 --> 00:01:00.827
(upbeat music)

00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:09.436 align:start size:94% position:6% line:0%
- I'm Dave Rubin and
this is The Rubin Report.

00:01:09.436 --> 00:01:12.238 align:start size:97% position:3% line:0%
Don't forget guys to subscribe
to our YouTube channel

00:01:12.238 --> 00:01:14.674 line:0%
and click that pesky
notification bell.

00:01:14.674 --> 00:01:16.976 line:0%
And joining me today
is a New York Times'

00:01:16.976 --> 00:01:18.545 line:0%
best selling author,

00:01:18.545 --> 00:01:21.581
the host of The Eric
Metaxas radio show

00:01:21.581 --> 00:01:25.218 align:start size:97% position:3%
and a senior fellow at
King's College in New York.

00:01:25.218 --> 00:01:28.121
Eric Metaxas, welcome
to The Rubin Report.

00:01:28.121 --> 00:01:29.556
- Thank you so much.

00:01:29.556 --> 00:01:31.658 align:start size:97% position:3%
I'm also now a senior
fellow at the Falkirk Center

00:01:31.658 --> 00:01:34.160
at Liberty University.

00:01:34.160 --> 00:01:36.696 align:start size:97% position:3% line:0%
Ever so slightly conservative
folks down there.

00:01:38.098 --> 00:01:40.667 align:start size:97% position:3% line:0%
- Can you be a senior fellow
at two different places?

00:01:40.667 --> 00:01:42.035 line:0%
Is that possible?

00:01:42.035 --> 00:01:43.536 align:start size:84% position:16% line:0%
Is that not a
conflict of interest?

00:01:43.536 --> 00:01:46.573 align:start size:97% position:3% line:0%
- If they don't listen to
this, then the answer is yes.

00:01:48.908 --> 00:01:50.176
- I hate to tell
you, but I do have

00:01:50.176 --> 00:01:51.811
a lot of fans at
Liberty University.

00:01:51.811 --> 00:01:54.514
I spoke there and they
could be watching this.

00:01:54.514 --> 00:01:58.218
- Can I tell you, no I love
them and I love Charlie Kirk

00:01:58.218 --> 00:02:01.955 line:0%
and I've just just done that.

00:02:01.955 --> 00:02:03.590 line:0%
Just become a senior
fellow with Falkirk

00:02:03.590 --> 00:02:05.758 line:0%
so it's a wonderful thing.

00:02:07.160 --> 00:02:08.895 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Well, listen man, I'm
very happy to have you here

00:02:08.895 --> 00:02:12.098
because I'm gonna do a
mea culpa right up front.

00:02:12.098 --> 00:02:14.667 align:start size:97% position:3%
I was on my book tour, as
you know, for about a month,

00:02:14.667 --> 00:02:16.803 align:start size:97% position:3%
just sitting in this chair,
being interviewed by,

00:02:16.803 --> 00:02:18.471 align:start size:84% position:16%
I probably did
hundreds of interviews

00:02:18.471 --> 00:02:20.340 align:start size:97% position:3%
and you were one of the
people that interviewed me.

00:02:20.340 --> 00:02:23.209 align:start position:0%
And I did not know who you
were before you interviewed me.

00:02:23.209 --> 00:02:24.611
I'm just saying it.

00:02:24.611 --> 00:02:27.313 align:start size:97% position:3%
I have now done a tremendous
amount of research.

00:02:27.313 --> 00:02:29.115 align:start size:84% position:16%
You've written
four billion books.

00:02:29.115 --> 00:02:30.984 align:start size:88% position:13%
I found you to be one
of the most interesting,

00:02:30.984 --> 00:02:34.821 align:start size:84% position:16%
insightful, curious
wordsmith interviewers

00:02:34.821 --> 00:02:36.422
of all the people
that I talked to,

00:02:36.422 --> 00:02:37.924
and I wanted to
return the favor.

00:02:37.924 --> 00:02:40.160
So that's just me getting
it out of the way here.

00:02:40.160 --> 00:02:42.095
- First of all, that
is so nice of you,

00:02:42.095 --> 00:02:43.863
and you didn't need to do that.

00:02:43.863 --> 00:02:45.365
That's like people say,

00:02:45.365 --> 00:02:47.567 align:start size:94% position:6%
"I had a really, really
hateful thought toward you

00:02:47.567 --> 00:02:49.802
seven years ago and I
wanted to confess it."

00:02:49.802 --> 00:02:51.571
And you think, "I wish
he hadn't told me that.

00:02:51.571 --> 00:02:53.106
Why did you have to do that?"

00:02:53.106 --> 00:02:55.041
No, I wouldn't expect you
to be aware of me, Dave,

00:02:55.041 --> 00:02:57.977
but you're very
kind to say that.

00:02:57.977 --> 00:02:59.746
And the listen,
here's the point,

00:02:59.746 --> 00:03:03.283
you're aware of me now, I'm
aware of you, we're all good.

00:03:03.283 --> 00:03:04.884
- We're also dressed the same

00:03:04.884 --> 00:03:07.420 align:start size:84% position:16%
and our hair is
sort of similar-ish,

00:03:07.420 --> 00:03:08.955
so we're in good company.

00:03:08.955 --> 00:03:11.324
But you're a really
established guy.

00:03:11.324 --> 00:03:12.992
You've done a ton
of great things

00:03:12.992 --> 00:03:14.427
and written several great books.

00:03:14.427 --> 00:03:18.097 align:start size:97% position:3%
So for anyone in my
audience that does not know

00:03:18.097 --> 00:03:21.100
who Eric Metaxas is, I just
read through your whole bio

00:03:21.100 --> 00:03:22.902
and all sorts of
interesting stuff.

00:03:22.902 --> 00:03:24.571
You were born in New York City.

00:03:24.571 --> 00:03:26.539
It seems like no one's actually

00:03:26.539 --> 00:03:28.174 align:start size:84% position:16%
born in New York City.
(indistinct)

00:03:28.174 --> 00:03:29.576 align:start size:97% position:3%
But very few people born there
- I had to be.

00:03:29.576 --> 00:03:31.077
I had to be.

00:03:31.077 --> 00:03:34.380
My mom came from Germany
on a boat in the mid '50s,

00:03:34.380 --> 00:03:36.916
my dad came from
Greece on a boat.

00:03:36.916 --> 00:03:40.119 align:start size:97% position:3%
They met in an English
class here in New York City

00:03:40.119 --> 00:03:41.888
to learn how to speak English.

00:03:41.888 --> 00:03:46.893 align:start size:97% position:3%
And when a Greek man is
involved in having a family,

00:03:48.261 --> 00:03:51.264 align:start size:97% position:3%
that kid has to be born
legally in Astoria, New York.

00:03:51.264 --> 00:03:53.233 align:start size:84% position:16%
So that's why, I
was born in Astoria,

00:03:53.233 --> 00:03:54.767
that's where I started life,

00:03:54.767 --> 00:03:57.370
Jackson Heights, Queens,
but I spent most of my life

00:03:57.370 --> 00:03:59.172
in Danbury, Connecticut.

00:03:59.172 --> 00:04:00.406
- Correct me if I'm wrong,

00:04:00.406 --> 00:04:02.375 align:start size:88% position:13%
you have to be born into
a bed of fettuccines.

00:04:02.375 --> 00:04:04.344 align:start size:84% position:16%
Is that true or-
- You know something,

00:04:04.344 --> 00:04:05.778
very few people know that.

00:04:05.778 --> 00:04:07.080
Very few people know that,

00:04:08.248 --> 00:04:09.949
because that's not
true, but still,

00:04:09.949 --> 00:04:11.718
I understand what you're saying.

00:04:11.718 --> 00:04:12.952
- Nonetheless.

00:04:12.952 --> 00:04:15.054 align:start size:81% position:19%
Okay, before we
get to your books,

00:04:15.054 --> 00:04:17.590
and I wanna talk about
your feelings on Trump

00:04:17.590 --> 00:04:19.058
and the culture wars,

00:04:19.058 --> 00:04:21.995 align:start size:97% position:3%
and we'll do some economics
and all sorts of stuff,

00:04:21.995 --> 00:04:24.097
tell me a little bit
about what got you

00:04:24.097 --> 00:04:27.700
into this talking world
of radio and writing,

00:04:27.700 --> 00:04:28.935
and all of that stuff.

00:04:28.935 --> 00:04:31.104
What is the ethos
of Eric Metaxas?

00:04:31.104 --> 00:04:33.706
- I've gotta give you
the super quick version

00:04:33.706 --> 00:04:35.675
'cause otherwise we'll
be here for the full hour

00:04:35.675 --> 00:04:36.909
talking about it.

00:04:36.909 --> 00:04:39.112
Basically, I am the
son of immigrants.

00:04:39.112 --> 00:04:43.049 align:start size:94% position:6%
My parents still have
accents and they raised me

00:04:43.049 --> 00:04:46.552
like most immigrants to
this country in the '50s

00:04:46.552 --> 00:04:48.354
and any time before the '60s.

00:04:48.354 --> 00:04:50.223
They raised me to love America

00:04:50.223 --> 00:04:53.126 align:start size:84% position:16%
because they knew
how wonderful it was

00:04:53.126 --> 00:04:54.594
having come from places.

00:04:54.594 --> 00:04:57.997 align:start size:97% position:3%
My mother escaped East Germany
when she was 17 years old

00:04:57.997 --> 00:05:01.401 align:start size:97% position:3%
because of the propaganda
coming to her in high school.

00:05:01.401 --> 00:05:05.571
My father saw the Civil War
with the communists in Greece

00:05:05.571 --> 00:05:06.873
in the early '50s.

00:05:06.873 --> 00:05:09.909
So they came here,
they love America,

00:05:09.909 --> 00:05:11.177
they taught me to love America

00:05:11.177 --> 00:05:13.146
and they raised me with basic

00:05:13.146 --> 00:05:15.915 align:start size:88% position:13%
homespun,
middle-American values.

00:05:15.915 --> 00:05:17.417
We went to church every Sunday,

00:05:17.417 --> 00:05:20.086
but we weren't born again or
evangelicals, if you're Greek.

00:05:20.086 --> 00:05:22.088
I always say, if
you're born Greek,

00:05:22.088 --> 00:05:23.790
if your parents are
Greek and German,

00:05:23.790 --> 00:05:26.092
you will be raised Greek.

00:05:26.092 --> 00:05:27.894
There's no option that
you're gonna be raised

00:05:27.894 --> 00:05:29.395
any other way except full Greek,

00:05:29.395 --> 00:05:31.397
so we went to church and stuff.

00:05:31.397 --> 00:05:34.400 align:start size:88% position:13%
And when I went to Yale,
the American dream,

00:05:34.400 --> 00:05:36.202 align:start size:84% position:16%
my parents didn't
get to go to college.

00:05:36.202 --> 00:05:37.503
I get to go to Yale University.

00:05:37.503 --> 00:05:39.238
Well, of course what happens,

00:05:39.238 --> 00:05:43.042
young Eric drinks the woke
Kool-Aid of the early '80s,

00:05:43.042 --> 00:05:46.479 align:start size:94% position:6%
and my world view, I would
have considered myself

00:05:46.479 --> 00:05:49.148 align:start size:97% position:3%
roughly speaking,
conservative and Christian.

00:05:49.148 --> 00:05:52.618
Before I know it I'm
taught, oh no, no, no,

00:05:52.618 --> 00:05:55.455 align:start size:97% position:3%
that's bigoted and
small-minded and parochial.

00:05:55.455 --> 00:05:59.225 align:start size:97% position:3%
So by the time I graduated,
I was totally lost.

00:05:59.225 --> 00:06:01.761
I was not exactly an
activist woke person,

00:06:01.761 --> 00:06:04.897 align:start size:88% position:13%
but the people political
correctness bug

00:06:04.897 --> 00:06:08.668
hit some of those top
institutions early

00:06:08.668 --> 00:06:12.505
and I was one of those
just confused people

00:06:12.505 --> 00:06:17.510 align:start size:97% position:3%
who had a vaguely
anti-American sense of things.

00:06:18.378 --> 00:06:19.612
I really betrayed all the things

00:06:19.612 --> 00:06:21.848
that my parents had taught me,

00:06:21.848 --> 00:06:24.584
and so I was very confused.

00:06:24.584 --> 00:06:26.419 align:start size:97% position:3%
Now, the one thing that
happened when I was in college

00:06:26.419 --> 00:06:29.155
is that I knew that I
wanted to be a writer.

00:06:29.155 --> 00:06:30.923
I originally thought I
want to go to politics,

00:06:30.923 --> 00:06:35.928 align:start position:0%
but I started reading the great
works of The Western Canon.

00:06:37.330 --> 00:06:38.297 align:start size:94% position:6%
And it's one of these
things, it's like a gene.

00:06:38.297 --> 00:06:40.199
I immediately said,

00:06:40.199 --> 00:06:41.768
"This is what I wanna do.

00:06:41.768 --> 00:06:44.771
I want to write, I love
words, I love ideas."

00:06:44.771 --> 00:06:46.806
But I didn't know what I
wanted to write exactly.

00:06:46.806 --> 00:06:50.309 align:start size:97% position:3%
So I won some awards at
Yale for my fiction writing.

00:06:50.309 --> 00:06:52.345
I was the editor of the
humor magazine there.

00:06:52.345 --> 00:06:54.380 align:start size:81% position:19%
I knew I had a
career in writing,

00:06:54.380 --> 00:06:58.518 align:start size:84% position:16%
but it really wasn't
until I graduated

00:06:58.518 --> 00:07:00.853
that I realized how lost I was.

00:07:00.853 --> 00:07:04.323
'Cause now you don't have
the guard rails of academia

00:07:04.323 --> 00:07:06.125
to keep you, this is
what I'm doing today.

00:07:06.125 --> 00:07:07.360
This is what I'm doing today.

00:07:07.360 --> 00:07:09.295
Suddenly I'm lost,
I wanna be a writer,

00:07:09.295 --> 00:07:13.232 align:start size:97% position:3%
but I don't have a
worldview other than I know,

00:07:13.232 --> 00:07:16.369 align:start size:81% position:19%
sort of how I'm
supposed to think.

00:07:16.369 --> 00:07:20.873 align:start size:88% position:13%
And I went through
some really tough years

00:07:20.873 --> 00:07:23.009
where I was just lost.

00:07:23.009 --> 00:07:26.579
I wasn't some bold sinner,
I was just totally lost.

00:07:26.579 --> 00:07:27.847
What's the meaning of life.

00:07:27.847 --> 00:07:29.482
Can you know whether
life has a meaning?

00:07:29.482 --> 00:07:30.683
Is there a God?

00:07:31.984 --> 00:07:34.687
I always say that if
you get a good job,

00:07:34.687 --> 00:07:36.389
you won't have time to
think about that stuff.

00:07:36.389 --> 00:07:38.591 align:start size:97% position:3%
And I think that's the
ethos of a place like Yale,

00:07:38.591 --> 00:07:39.792
all these institutions,

00:07:39.792 --> 00:07:42.628 align:start size:88% position:13%
their idea is we ceased
about 100 years ago

00:07:42.628 --> 00:07:44.397
answering the big questions.

00:07:44.397 --> 00:07:45.631
What's the meaning of life?

00:07:45.631 --> 00:07:47.033
Is there a God?

00:07:47.033 --> 00:07:48.267
What's the meaning of your life?

00:07:48.267 --> 00:07:49.569
Where do you go when you die?

00:07:49.569 --> 00:07:50.870
Where do we come from?

00:07:50.870 --> 00:07:51.704
We don't go there.

00:07:51.704 --> 00:07:53.206
They don't go there.

00:07:53.206 --> 00:07:54.907
So what they tell you is like,

00:07:54.907 --> 00:07:57.143
study really hard,
get a great job,

00:07:57.143 --> 00:08:00.079 align:start size:97% position:3%
and you'll be able to distract
yourself for a few decades

00:08:00.079 --> 00:08:01.047
and then it'll all be over.

00:08:01.047 --> 00:08:02.315
So just do whatever you do.

00:08:02.315 --> 00:08:03.749
Don't think about
the big questions.

00:08:03.749 --> 00:08:05.451 align:start size:97% position:3%
Well, I was an English
major, I wanna be a writer,

00:08:05.451 --> 00:08:06.285
guess what happened?

00:08:06.285 --> 00:08:07.753
I didn't get a good job.

00:08:07.753 --> 00:08:11.023 align:start size:97% position:3%
I had several years to think
about the big questions.

00:08:11.023 --> 00:08:14.861
And it got so bad
that I ended up

00:08:14.861 --> 00:08:16.462
moving back in with my parents.

00:08:16.462 --> 00:08:18.664
If your parents are
European immigrants,

00:08:18.664 --> 00:08:21.534 align:start position:0%
working-class European
immigrants who sent you to Yale,

00:08:21.534 --> 00:08:23.870
you do not wanna move
back in with them.

00:08:23.870 --> 00:08:25.104 align:start size:84% position:16%
Trust me on this one.
- Right.

00:08:25.104 --> 00:08:27.039
- It was pure hell.

00:08:27.039 --> 00:08:29.075
And in the midst of that hell,

00:08:29.075 --> 00:08:31.244
I did the only thing
that you could do,

00:08:31.244 --> 00:08:34.747 align:start size:84% position:16%
which is I got a job
as a proofreader,

00:08:34.747 --> 00:08:37.283 align:start size:97% position:3%
at Union Carbide Corporation
in Danbury, Connecticut.

00:08:37.283 --> 00:08:38.684
I'm living with my parents

00:08:38.684 --> 00:08:41.220 align:start size:97% position:3%
and I'm going to a chemical
conglomerate to do,

00:08:41.220 --> 00:08:42.588
like, it was pure hell.

00:08:42.588 --> 00:08:44.891
And in the midst of that hell,

00:08:44.891 --> 00:08:47.393 align:start size:88% position:13%
I met a guy who was
pretty serious Christian

00:08:47.393 --> 00:08:49.629
and he begins sharing
his faith with me.

00:08:49.629 --> 00:08:53.032 align:start size:84% position:16%
And I had the standard
Yale response like,

00:08:53.032 --> 00:08:55.902 align:start size:84% position:16%
don't get too close
with that weird stuff.

00:08:55.902 --> 00:08:59.305
But I was in enough pain that
I was listening a little bit.

00:08:59.305 --> 00:09:01.140
And this kind of cat
and mouse game went on

00:09:01.140 --> 00:09:02.441
for about a year.

00:09:02.441 --> 00:09:05.444 align:start size:88% position:13%
And I would say
roughly a year into it,

00:09:05.444 --> 00:09:07.513
the cat to the chase,

00:09:07.513 --> 00:09:11.484 align:start size:84% position:16%
I had a very dramatic
conversion dream.

00:09:11.484 --> 00:09:13.920
It was one of those things
like it's out of a book.

00:09:13.920 --> 00:09:18.925
It was a miracle, nothing else
could've sufficed to drag me

00:09:20.626 --> 00:09:23.296
across the broomstick into
another universe basically

00:09:23.296 --> 00:09:24.964 align:start size:88% position:13%
'cause I thought all
those people are insane.

00:09:24.964 --> 00:09:28.200 align:start size:84% position:16%
So I had this
miraculous conversion

00:09:28.200 --> 00:09:32.438
and I've spent the last 30
years trying to process this,

00:09:32.438 --> 00:09:33.739
reading books and stuff.

00:09:33.739 --> 00:09:35.541
But it was because of that

00:09:35.541 --> 00:09:37.777 align:start size:84% position:16%
that I very quickly
became a conservative.

00:09:37.777 --> 00:09:40.479
I think it was the pro-life
issue and a few other issues

